Now I understand the bug. Let me analyze the key issue:

In `add_complete_typeobj` (and `add_minimal_typeobj`), for STRUCTURE and UNION, when `ddsi_type_register_dep` fails at member `n`, the cleanup loop is:
```c
for (uint32_t m = 0; m < n - 1; m++)
```
This should be `m < n`. When `n = 0`, `n - 1` underflows to `UINT32_MAX`, causing the loop to iterate over uninitialized memory (members.seq was calloc'd, so they're NULL, and `ddsi_type_unref_locked` on NULL would crash with assert). When `n > 0`, member `n-1` is skipped in cleanup.

But wait - the key issue for the UAF: When `ddsi_type_register_dep` fails at member `n`, the cleanup unrefs members `0..n-2` (missing `n-1`), then frees `members.seq`, and sets `xt->_d = TK_NONE`. 

But here's the thing: `ddsi_type_register_dep` for member `n` may have PARTIALLY succeeded. Let me look at `ddsi_type_register_dep_impl` again:

```c
static dds_return_t ddsi_type_register_dep_impl (...)
{
  ...
  bool existing = ddsrt_avl_lookup (&ddsi_typedeps_treedef, &gv->typedeps, dep) != NULL;
  if (!existing)
  {
    dep->from_type_info = from_type_info;
    ddsrt_avl_insert (&ddsi_typedeps_treedef, &gv->typedeps, dep);
    ddsrt_avl_insert (&ddsi_typedeps_reverse_treedef, &gv->typedeps_reverse, dep);
    if ((ret = ddsi_type_ref_id_locked (gv, dst_dep_type, &dep_type_id)) != DDS_RETCODE_OK)
    {
      ddsrt_avl_delete (&ddsi_typedeps_treedef, &gv->typedeps, dep);
      ddsrt_avl_delete (&ddsi_typedeps_reverse_treedef, &gv->typedeps_reverse, dep);
    }
  }
  ...
}
```

When `ddsi_type_ref_id_locked` fails, the dep is removed from the trees but `dep` is NOT freed (memory leak), and `dst_dep_type` is set to NULL. So `xt->_u.structure.members.seq[n].type` remains NULL (from calloc).

